# Modus Accordion/Collapse State Management

## 🚨 Common Issue: Accordion Not Expanding/Collapsing

### ❌ The Problem

When using Modus Web Components like `ModusWcAccordion` and `ModusWcCollapse`, the expand/collapse functionality may not work properly if you try to control the state from React.

**Symptoms:**

- Accordion headers appear but clicking doesn't expand/collapse items
- Event handlers fire but UI doesn't update
- Components render but are not interactive

### 🔍 Root Cause

**State Management Conflict**: Modus Web Components manage their own internal state, and trying to control this from React creates conflicts.

```tsx
// ❌ WRONG - This causes the accordion to not work
<ModusWcCollapse
  expanded={isExpanded} // React state conflicts with internal state
  options={item.options}
>
  <div slot="content">Content</div>
</ModusWcCollapse>
```

### ✅ The Solution

**Let Modus Web Components handle their own state:**

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - No expanded prop, let component manage its own state
<ModusWcCollapse
  options={item.options} // Only pass configuration
>
  <div slot="content">Content</div>
</ModusWcCollapse>
```

### 🎯 Best Practices

#### 1. Event Listening (Not Control)

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Listen to events for tracking/analytics
useEffect(() => {
  const accordion = accordionRef.current;
  if (accordion) {
    const handleExpandedChange = (event: Event) => {
      const customEvent = event as CustomEvent<{
        expanded: boolean;
        index: number;
      }>;
      // Track for logging/analytics, don't try to control
      console.log(
        "Item",
        customEvent.detail.index,
        "is",
        customEvent.detail.expanded ? "open" : "closed"
      );
    };

    accordion.addEventListener("expandedChange", handleExpandedChange);
    return () =>
      accordion.removeEventListener("expandedChange", handleExpandedChange);
  }
}, []);
```

#### 2. Component Structure

```tsx
// ✅ CORRECT - Proper accordion implementation
export default function ModusAccordion({ items, onExpandedChange }) {
  const accordionRef = useRef<HTMLModusWcAccordionElement>(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const accordion = accordionRef.current;
    if (accordion && onExpandedChange) {
      const handleExpandedChange = (event: Event) => {
        const customEvent = event as CustomEvent<{
          expanded: boolean;
          index: number;
        }>;
        onExpandedChange(customEvent.detail);
      };

      accordion.addEventListener("expandedChange", handleExpandedChange);
      return () =>
        accordion.removeEventListener("expandedChange", handleExpandedChange);
    }
  }, [onExpandedChange]);

  return (
    <ModusWcAccordion ref={accordionRef}>
      {items.map((item) => (
        <ModusWcCollapse
          key={item.id}
          collapse-id={item.id}
          options={item.options} // No expanded prop
        >
          <div slot="content">{item.content}</div>
        </ModusWcCollapse>
      ))}
    </ModusWcAccordion>
  );
}
```

### 🚫 What NOT to Do

```tsx
// ❌ DON'T - Control state from React
const [expandedItems, setExpandedItems] = useState([0]);

<ModusWcCollapse
  expanded={expandedItems.includes(index)}  // This breaks functionality
  options={item.options}
>

// ❌ DON'T - Use React synthetic events
<ModusWcCollapse
  onExpandedChange={(event) => {}}  // This won't work
>

// ❌ DON'T - Try to programmatically control expansion
useEffect(() => {
  if (shouldExpand) {
    collapseRef.current.expanded = true;  // This won't work reliably
  }
}, [shouldExpand]);
```

### ✅ What TO Do

```tsx
// ✅ DO - Let components handle their own state
<ModusWcCollapse
  options={item.options}  // Only configuration
>

// ✅ DO - Use native DOM events
accordion.addEventListener("expandedChange", handleExpandedChange);

// ✅ DO - Track state for analytics/logging only
const handleExpandedChange = (event: Event) => {
  const customEvent = event as CustomEvent<{expanded: boolean; index: number}>;
  // Log for analytics, don't try to control
  analytics.track('accordion_toggle', {
    index: customEvent.detail.index,
    expanded: customEvent.detail.expanded
  });
};
```

### 🔧 Debugging Tips

1. **Check Console**: Look for event logs to see if events are firing
2. **Verify Event Listeners**: Ensure `addEventListener` is properly set up
3. **Remove State Props**: Remove any `expanded` props from Modus components
4. **Use Native Events**: Use `addEventListener` instead of React event handlers

### 📋 Checklist

- [ ] ✅ **No `expanded` props** on Modus Web Components
- [ ] ✅ **Event listeners** use `addEventListener` not React handlers
- [ ] ✅ **State tracking** for analytics only, not control
- [ ] ✅ **Native DOM events** for Modus component interactions
- [ ] ✅ **Component refs** for accessing DOM elements
- [ ] ✅ **Cleanup listeners** in useEffect return function

---

**Remember**: Modus Web Components are designed to be self-contained. Let them manage their own state and only listen to their events for tracking purposes.
